<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened Runtime entitlements for the Juno.app main bundle.

  Every entitlement here weakens a macOS protection, so the list is the set the
  app demonstrably needs and nothing else. What is ABSENT is as deliberate as
  what is present; see the notes at the bottom before adding anything.

  Nested helpers (Juno Helper.app, Juno Helper (Renderer).app, GPU, and the
  utilityProcess host) are signed with entitlements.mac.inherit.plist instead.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>

    <!--
      REQUIRED. V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at runtime and marks
      those pages executable, which the Hardened Runtime forbids by default.
      Without this, the app launches and immediately crashes.

      This is the one entitlement @electron/notarize lists as a hard
      prerequisite for notarizing an Electron app, and the only runtime
      exception in @electron/osx-sign's current default Darwin entitlements.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      REQUIRED (pragmatically). Library validation restricts the process to
      loading code signed by the same Team ID or by Apple.

      Juno loads node-pty, a native N-API addon whose `.node` binary and
      `spawn-helper` executable arrive as prebuilt artifacts from npm rather
      than being built and signed here. electron-builder re-signs binaries it
      finds in the bundle, but prebuilt native modules are the long-standing
      exception that breaks under library validation
      (electron-userland/electron-builder#3940), which is why electron-builder's
      own default entitlements template still carries this key.

      This is the entitlement most worth trying to drop later. The test is
      cheap and specific: remove it, produce a signed build, open a terminal
      pane, and see whether node-pty loads. Do not drop it on the basis that
      the unsigned local build works — library validation is not enforced there.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      REQUIRED for voice dictation. Under the Hardened Runtime, microphone
      access needs BOTH this entitlement and a TCC grant from the user; the
      entitlement alone grants nothing, and its absence makes the TCC prompt
      moot. Pairs with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription, set via `mac.extendInfo`
      in electron-builder.yml — macOS terminates a process that triggers the
      prompt without a purpose string.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      ── Deliberately NOT requested ──────────────────────────────────────────

      com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        Needed only by Electron 11 and earlier. @electron/notarize's
        prerequisites are explicit: "If you are using Electron 11 or below, you
        must add [it] too. When using version 12+, this entitlement should not
        be applied as it increases your app's attack surface." This app is on
        Electron 43. It is also gone from @electron/osx-sign's current defaults.
        electron-builder's bundled template still includes it — that template is
        stale, which is exactly why this file is checked in rather than
        inherited.

      com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
        Lets DYLD_* variables inject code into THIS process. Juno never needs to
        be affected by them: it spawns agent CLIs, node-pty shells and
        utilityProcess children as separate processes, each subject to its own
        signature and its own entitlements. Add this only if Juno itself must
        run under a profiler or an injected dylib — a development need, not a
        shipping one. Not in @electron/osx-sign's defaults either.

      Spawning child processes
        There is no Hardened Runtime entitlement for this, and none is needed.
        `posix_spawn`/`exec` of node-pty's spawn-helper, of agent CLIs, and of
        Electron's utilityProcess are unrestricted. What child processes
        actually require is operational, not declarative: every Mach-O inside
        the bundle must be signed (electron-builder does this), spawn-helper
        must keep its executable bit, and native code must be unpacked out of
        app.asar. All three are handled in electron-builder.yml.

      Screen recording and Accessibility
        These are TCC permissions granted by the user at runtime in System
        Settings › Privacy & Security. They are NOT entitlements. There is no
        `com.apple.security.accessibility`, and no screen-recording entitlement
        of any spelling — nor is there an Info.plist purpose string for either
        (NSScreenCaptureUsageDescription and NSAccessibilityUsageDescription do
        not exist; Apple's property-list reference 404s on both, while
        NSMicrophoneUsageDescription resolves). Plenty of third-party writing
        claims otherwise. Inventing plausible-looking keys would assert a
        control that does not exist, which is strictly worse than the silence,
        so this paragraph is the record that the absence was decided rather than
        overlooked.

      com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
        Only if Juno drives other apps via AppleScript / Apple Events. It does
        not today. Would also need NSAppleEventsUsageDescription.

      com.apple.security.get-task-allow
        Debug-only. Must never ship: it lets any process attach a debugger to
        Juno, and notarization rejects it.
    -->

  </dict>
</plist>
